+    basis: "review/code rubric: idempotency of external writes; deterministic where it claims to be"
+    severity: P3
+    file: core/components/skills/track-plan-changes/scripts/tracker.py
+    function: HostTracker.find_marked
+    line: 212
+    artifact_type: garura-prose
+    evidence: >-
+      The rerun safety of a create rests on the host's issue search (`list-issues` with the marker as an
+      in:body phrase, 10 results). No test runs this path: test_track_changes.py uses only the file stand-in,
+      whose find_marked scans its own data directly. I cannot show from the code that an issue made seconds
+      earlier is already found by the host's search, so a rerun right after a crash may not find it and may
+      make the issue twice. The search is the only guard left for the case where the record was never written.
+      Fix: test HostTracker.find_marked against a fake adapter, and say in the skill what the person does when a
+      create is rerun at once after a crash (look in the tracker before the rerun).
+  - status: new
+    basis: "review/code rubric: safe handling of destructive/irreversible operations"
+    severity: P4
+    file: core/tools/plan/plan_branch.py
+    function: go
+    line: 183
+    artifact_type: runtime-code
+    evidence: >-
+      The plan file is opened for writing (which empties it) before set_fields() has run, so a plan file with no
+      `status:` line is emptied first and put back only by the `git checkout -- path` in the stop handler (line
+      199). It is safe today (I ran it: the file came back byte for byte), but it depends on that handler, and
+      the handler catches only Stop: an OSError on the write would leave main's plan file changed. Fix: compute
+      the new text before opening the file.
+  - status: new
+    basis: "review/code rubric: no machine path or user name"
+    severity: P4
+    file: core/tools/lint-components/index.js
+    function: main
+    line: 95
+    artifact_type: runtime-code
+    evidence: >-
+      The report drops only the working-folder prefix (`process.cwd() + path.sep`). Run from the repository root,
+      as the play does, the report is clean. Run from another folder with --target elsewhere, I got 37
+      absolute paths in the output, each starting with the home folder. The comment says the aim is a report that
+      can be committed without naming the machine; that holds only from the root. Also the replace is applied
+      anywhere inside a string, not only at its start. Fix: strip the prefix only at the start of a path, and say
+      in the usage text that the report is relative to the working folder only.
+  - status: new
+    basis: "review/code rubric: deterministic contract between steps"
+    severity: P4
+    file: core/components/plays/update-plan/scripts/commit_plan.py
+    function: main
+    line: 83
+    artifact_type: garura-prose
+    evidence: >-
+      The new check refuses every absolute path and every path that leaves the repository, and git add runs
+      from the repository root. The play says `model-record.json` holds "every model file written" (SKILL.md
+      line 267) without saying the paths are repository-relative, while link-model-part reports `changed`
+      relative to the product base (link-model-part/SKILL.md line 46). I did not run the real hand-off, so I
+      do not know which form the keeper writes. If it passes the product-base form, git add fails on a path
+      that does not exist from the root and the commit step refuses every time. Fix: state in SKILL.md that
+      `files` is repository-relative.
