++      stands. After the revert is pushed, a refused branch delete (line 253 or 255) raises from git() with
++      check=True, so the session stays on main (back() is not reached) and the record is unchanged. I
++      reproduced it in a throwaway repo with a remote that refuses deletes: main holds the revert, the remote
++      still holds plan/<id>/base, and a second `abort` stops with exit 2 and the message "clashes with later
++      work", because `git revert` of a commit that is already reverted fails (lines 241-246 only prove the cut is
